    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d489">

                <pb facs="0843" n="723" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg723"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/4486: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Vice Consul in Charge at Canton</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Huston</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Canton</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-06-22T11:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 22,
                                1922—11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received June 23—11:15
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Wu Ting-fang<anchor n="57" xml:id="d489-f1"/> handed over seal of office to
                    Provincial Assembly on 21st. On the same day Assembly elected Chen as
                    Provisional Governor of Kwangtung. On 22nd Chen wired Wu Ting-fang asking <pb
                        facs="0844" n="724" xml:id="pg724"/> him to persuade Sun to retire for the
                    sake of unification. Agreement between Chen’s officers and navy. The navy will
                    request retirement Sun and submit to Chen’s authority. Sun still at Whampoa. He
                    hopes expeditionary force can fight way back Canton. A few followers advising
                    him retire. Wu Ting-fang died at one this morning [of] pneumonia after brief
                    illness.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Huston</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d489-f1" n="57" xml:id="d489-f1-content">
                <p>Minister of Foreign Affairs, Canton Government.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
